====Front for Victory====

Initially, the FPV had several pre-candidates to the presidency, but only Daniel Scioli and [[Florencio Randazzo]] had a good reception in the opinion polls. Scioli was resisted by factions of the party that did not consider him truly loyal to Kirchner. All the minor candidates resigned when Kirchner asked them to do so.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1790706-cristina-kirchner-en-chaco|title= Cristina Kirchner quiere menos candidatos a presidente en el PJ: pidió "un baño de humildad"|trans-title= Cristina Kirchner wants less presidential candidates in the PJ: she asked for a "bath of humility"|language= es|date= 7 May 2015|newspaper= La Nación|access-date=1 September 2015}}</ref> Randazzo resigned as well some weeks before the primary elections, leaving Scioli as the sole precandidate of the FPV.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.perfil.com/politica/Cedio-Randazzo-descarto-su-intento-por-la-Presidencia--20150617-0026.html|title= Cedió Randazzo: descartó su intento por la Presidencia|trans-title= Randazzo gave it up: he discarded his attempt for the presidency|language= es|date= 15 June 2015|publisher= Perfil|access-date=1 September 2015}}</ref> Randazzo did not accept to run for governor of the Buenos Aires province, which had primary elections between minister [[Aníbal Fernández]] and [[Julián Domínguez]].<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1803134-la-negativa-de-randazzo-reabrio-la-disputa-por-la-gobernacion|title= La negativa de Randazzo reabrió la disputa por la gobernación|trans-title= Randazzo's refusal reopened the dispute for the governorship|language= es|author= Mariano Obarrio|date= 19 June 2015|newspaper= La Nación|access-date=1 September 2015}}</ref> Fernández won the local primary elections.

