===Provincial governors===
{{main|2015 Argentine provincial elections}}
On 25 October elections numerous provinces also elected governors, with the new ones beginning their terms on 10 December 2015. These provinces were [[Buenos Aires province]], [[Catamarca province|Catamarca]], [[Chubut province|Chubut]], [[Entre Ríos province|Entre Ríos]], [[Formosa province|Formosa]], [[Jujuy province|Jujuy]], [[La Pampa Province|La Pampa]], [[Misiones province|Misiones]], [[San Juan Province, Argentina|San Juan]], [[San Luis province|San Luis]] and [[Santa Cruz Province, Argentina|Santa Cruz]], encompassing 11 of the country's 23 provinces. The other provinces elected governors in different days of 2015; the only exceptions were Corrientes and Santiago del Estero whose governors' terms were not due to finish in 2015.<ref name="gobernador">{{cite news|title=Elecciones 2015: los resultados de las provincias que eligieron gobernador|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1839651-cuales-son-los-resultados-de-las-provincias-que-eligieron-gobernador|access-date=27 October 2015|date=26 October 2015}}</ref>

[[María Eugenia Vidal]] was elected governor of the populous [[Buenos Aires Province]], defeating the controversial [[Aníbal Fernández]]; her victory influenced as well the growth of Macri in the presidential elections. Similarly, the unpopular Fernández may have subtracted non-Kirchnerite votes from Scioli.<ref name="gobernador"/> The victory was considered significant given that Fernández was the incumbent [[Chief of the Cabinet of Ministers|Cabinet Chief]] of the Kirchner administration, and that Vidal was directly replacing [[Daniel Scioli]]'s post as governor of the province.<ref name=Jastreblansky>{{cite news|last1=Jastreblansky|first1=Maia|title=El "no" a Aníbal, en números: corte de boleta y distritos que cambiaron de color|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1839973-resultados-provincia-anibal-vidal|access-date=28 October 2015|work=La Nacion|date=26 October 2015}}</ref> It is made more significant as she is the first female governor of the province, and the first non-Peronist governor since 1987. The [[Republican Proposal]] also retained the city of [[Buenos Aires]], that elected [[Horacio Rodríguez Larreta]] as the new mayor.<ref name="gobernador"/> The PRO stronghold had gone to a second round between Larreta and [[Martín Lousteau]] (also of the [[Cambiemos]] front, but not in the same party) after the [[Front for Victory]]'s [[Mariano Recalde]] finished third.<ref>[http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1807919-elecciones-portenas-2015-amplio-triunfo-de-horacio-rodriguez-larreta-pero-habria-ballottage-con-martin-lousteau Elecciones porteñas 2015: amplio triunfo de Horacio Rodríguez Larreta, pero habrá ballottage con Martín Lousteau] - La Nacion, 5 July 2015</ref> [[Juan Schiaretti]] won the elections in [[Córdoba Province, Argentina|Córdoba]], and he is the single governor of the UNA ticket.<ref name="gobernador"/> The socialist [[Miguel Lifschitz]] was elected governor of [[Santa Fe province|Santa Fe]], after a controversial triple tie with the PJ and PRO. [[Carlos Verna]] was reelected governor of [[La Pampa Province|La Pampa]].<ref name="gobernador"/>

The votes in [[Entre Ríos Province|Entre Ríos]] had a slow count. During a week, the provisional results suggested that [[Gustavo Bordet]] may be the new governor of Entre Ríos, but [[Alfredo de Angeli]] claimed that the uncounted votes may turn the tide and make him the winner instead.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://supercampo.perfil.com/2015/10/de-angeli-cerca-de-la-gobernacion-de-entre-rios/|title= De Ángeli, cerca de la gobernación de Entre Ríos|trans-title= De Ángeli near being governor of Río Negro|language= es|date= 27 October 2015|publisher= Perfil|access-date=27 October 2015}}</ref> The final results were released on 30 October, confirming the victory of Bordet. In [[Misiones province|Misiones]], [[Hugo Passalacqua]], vice governor of [[Maurice Closs]] was elected governor by a wide margin.<ref name="gobernador"/>

[[Rosana Bertone]] was elected governor of [[Tierra del Fuego Province, Argentina|Tierra del Fuego]]. The radical [[Eduardo Costa (politician)|Eduardo Costa]] got the higher number of votes in [[Santa Cruz Province, Argentina|Santa Cruz]], but [[Alicia Kirchner]] was elected governor, thanks to the [[Ley de Lemas]]. The former governor of [[Chubut Province|Chubut]] [[Mario Das Neves]] was elected again. [[Neuquén province|Neuquén]] and [[Río Negro province|Río Negro]] elected [[Omar Gutiérrez]] and [[Alberto Weretilneck]], who ran for local parties.<ref name="gobernador"/>

[[Mendoza province|Mendoza]] provided an early victory for the opposition, by the radical [[Alfredo Cornejo (politician)|Alfredo Cornejo]]. [[Alberto Rodríguez Saá]] was elected governor of [[San Luis province|San Luis]] once again. The Front for Victory retained the provinces of [[San Juan Province, Argentina|San Juan]] and [[La Rioja Province, Argentina|La Rioja]], with [[Sergio Uñac]] and [[Sergio Casas]].<ref name="gobernador"/>

[[Lucía Corpacci]] was reelected in [[Catamarca province|Catamarca]] and [[Gildo Insfrán]] was reelected in [[Formosa province|Formosa]]. In [[Chaco Province|Chaco]], [[Domingo Peppo]] was elected governor. [[Juan Manuel Urtubey]] got an important victory in [[Salta province|Salta]] against Romero, and kept the province for the FPV. The radical [[Gerardo Morales (politician)|Gerardo Morales]] was elected governor of [[Jujuy province|Jujuy]], the first non-Peronist one since the return of democracy in 1983. He expects to have a tense relation with the populist [[Milagro Sala]]. [[Juan Luis Manzur]] was elected governor of [[Tucumán province|Tucumán]], but the denounces of electoral fraud became a national scandal. The elections were first declared null by  local judge, and then ratified by the local Supreme Court. The case is currently held by the national Supreme Court.<ref name="gobernador"/><ref>{{cite web |url= http://newsghana.com.gh/points-worth-noting-about-argentinas-presidential-election/|title=Points worth noting about Argentina's presidential election|language= en|author= News Ghana |date= 22 November 2015|publisher= News Ghana|access-date=22 November 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url= http://newsghana.com.gh/argentinas-daniel-scioli-losses-in-presidential-race/|title=Argentina's Daniel Scioli losses in presidential race|language= en|author= News Ghana |date= 22 November 2015|publisher= News Ghana|access-date=22 November 2015}}</ref>

==International reaction==

===Americas===
*{{Flagu|Brazil}} – Despite the ruling [[Workers' Party (Brazil)|Workers' party]] having supported Scioli during the campaign, Brazilian president [[Dilma Rousseff]] congratulated Macri and invited him to a state visit "as soon as possible", while she is also set to attend Macri's inauguration as president. The pair have stated that improving bilateral relations between the two countries, as well as strengthening the [[Mercosur]] trade bloc.<ref name=Armendariz>{{cite news|last1=Armendariz|first1=Alberto|title=Dilma Rousseff felicitó a Macri y lo invitó a reunirse en Brasilia|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848145-dilma-rousseff-asistira-a-la-asuncion-de-mauricio-macri|access-date=23 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Chile}} – Chilean president [[Michelle Bachelet]] contacted Macri by phone and spoke about the importance for both countries which can maintain the spirit of cooperation, integration and development which characterizes their common history and the importance of further work for Latin America.<ref>{{cite news|title=Presidenta de Chile se comunico con Mauricio Macri|url=http://www.telam.com.ar/notas/201511/128101-mandatarios-felicitacion-macri-triunfo.html|access-date=23 November 2015|work=Telam|date=22 November 2015|archive-date=25 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151125171815/http://www.telam.com.ar/notas/201511/128101-mandatarios-felicitacion-macri-triunfo.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Colombia}} – Colombian president [[Juan Manuel Santos]] expressed "Congratulations to Mauricio Macri for his victory in presidential elections in Argentina. Successes in his management. It has our full support".<ref>{{cite news|title=Juan Manuel Santos felicitó a Mauricio Macri|url=http://www.infobae.com/2015/11/22/1771636-lideres-la-region-felicitan-mauricio-macri-los-resultados-la-eleccion-argentina|access-date=24 November 2015|work=Infobae|date=22 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Ecuador}} – Ecuadorian president [[Rafael Correa]] congratulated Macri for his victory and wished him "the best of luck". While commenting on the presidencies of the Kirchners, he stated that "12 years ago Argentina was reborn like a phoenix, after [[neoliberalism]] had left it in ashes" whilst thanking the incumbent Cristina Fernández de Kirchner.<ref>{{cite news|title=Rafael Correa felicitó a Mauricio Macri por la victoria en el ballottage|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848159-rafael-correa-felicito-a-mauricio-macri-por-la-victoria-en-el-ballottage|access-date=23 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Mexico}} – Mexican president [[Enrique Peña Nieto]] stated that "Mexico will work with" Macri's government to strengthen "bilateral relations and the wellbeing of Latin America".<ref name=AlianzadelPacifico />
*{{Flagu|Peru}} – Peruvian President [[Ollanta Humala]] contacted with Macri in order to congratulate him on his election victory and point out that the Peruvian Government has "strong will" to strengthen ties with his country, reported the Peruvian Foreign Ministry.<ref>{{cite news|title=Humala a Macri: Avancemos en la agenda bilateral entre el Perú y Argentina|url=http://www.americatv.com.pe/noticias/actualidad/ollanta-humala-pide-humberto-macri-avanzar-agenda-bilateral-entre-peru-y-argentina-n208082|access-date=23 November 2015|work=Americatv|date=23 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|United States}} – The [[United States Secretary of State]] [[John Kerry]] congratulated the country for its "successful elections", adding that he was "looking forward to working closely" with Macri and his government.<ref>{{cite news|title=John Kerry: "Espero trabajar en estrecha colaboración con Mauricio Macri"|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848193-john-kerry-espero-trabajar-en-estrecha-colaboracion-con-mauricio-macri|access-date=24 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref> Meanwhile, [[United States Ambassador to Argentina]] [[Noah Mamet]] wished Macri well.<ref name=AlianzadelPacifico>{{cite news|title=Macri anticipó su agenda internacional: Brasil y la Alianza del Pacífico|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848096-macri-anticipo-su-agenda-internacional-brasil-y-la-alianza-del-pacifico|access-date=24 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref> Members of the [[United States House of Representatives]] later asked [[Barack Obama]] in a letter to prioritise US-Argentine relations during 2016, stating that "The United States and Argentina should be natural partners. Both have highly educated populations, diversified economies and vast natural resources" and calling such a relationship a "win-win" for both countries. The letter also stressed the importance of reversing high levels of [[anti-americanism]] in the country and resolving the holdout problem with the [[vulture fund]]s, among other key issues.<ref>{{cite news|title=Tras el triunfo de Macri, legisladores de EE.UU. le piden a Barack Obama que "priorice las relaciones con la Argentina"|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848727-legisladores-de-eeuu-le-mandaron-una-carta-a-mauricio-macri-para-pedirle-que-priorice-las-relaciones-con-eeuu|access-date=26 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=25 November 2015}}</ref> Obama later congratulated Macri personally, while an official [[White House]] statement confirmed that the President intends to strengthen ties.<ref>{{cite news|title=Barack Obama felicitó a Mauricio Macri y se comprometió a trabajar en el sector energético|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848852-barack-obama-felicito-a-mauricio-macri|access-date=26 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=25 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Uruguay}} – Uruguayan president [[Tabaré Vázquez]] greeted Mauricio Macri in a telephone interview for his victory in presidential runoff and asked him to convey the congratulations to the people of Argentina for the civic maturity demonstrated during the election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Vázquez felicitó a Macri|url=http://www.republica.com.uy/vazquez-felicito-a-macri/547790/|access-date=24 November 2015|work=Republica|date=24 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Venezuela}} – On 23 November, [[Venezuela]]'s [[Democratic Unity Roundtable|opposition]] hailed Macri's presidential win in Argentina as a blow for leftists in Latin America and a good omen for their own duel with "Chavismo" in [[2015 Venezuelan parliamentary election|next month's parliamentary vote]]. "That was a big disappointment for Venezuela's ruling socialist "Chavismo" movement, which had a close political alliance with Fernández."<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.reuters.com/article/us-argentina-election-venezuela-oppositi-idUSKBN0TC1AQ20151123#r4Ihez7viCostruJ.97|title= Venezuela opposition cheer Macri's Argentina presidential win|date= 23 November 2013|publisher= Reuters|access-date=23 November 2015}}</ref> [[Diosdado Cabello]] called Macri a "[[Fascist (insult)|fascist]]", and asked him to stay away of Venezuelan internal affairs, as Macri had proposed to remove Venezuela from the Mercosur because of the treatment to [[Leopoldo López]] and other political prisoners.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1849024-diosdado-cabello-el-hombre-fuerte-de-venezuela-llamo-fascista-a-macri-y-le-advirtio-no-se-meta-con-nosotros|title= Diosdado Cabello, el hombre fuerte de Venezuela, llamó "fascista" a Macri y le advirtió: "No se meta con nosotros"|trans-title= Diosdado Cabello, the strongman of Venezuela, called Macri a "fascist" and warned: "Do not mess with us"|language= es|agency= Reuters|date= 26 November 2015|publisher= La Nación|access-date=23 November 2015}}</ref>

===Asia===
*{{Flagu|China}} – "China congratulates Mr. Macri on being elected as the new Argentinean president and wishes the Argentinean people new achievements in their national development," Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman [[Hong Lei (politician)|Hong Lei]] said at a regular press briefing.<ref>{{cite news|title=China congratulates Macri on winning Argentinean presidential election|url=http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2015-11/23/c_134846420.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151124214934/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2015-11/23/c_134846420.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=24 November 2015|access-date=23 November 2015|work=Xinhuanet|date=23 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Israel}} – Prime Minister [[Benjamin Netanyahu]] says he hopes ties between two countries to strengthen, invites Mauricio Macri to visit [[Israel]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Netanyahu Congratulates Argentine President-elect Macri|url=https://www.haaretz.com/israel-news/1.688420|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151126022712/http://www.haaretz.com/israel-news/1.688420|url-status=dead|archive-date=26 November 2015|access-date=25 November 2015|work=[[Haaretz]]|date=25 November 2015}}</ref>

===Europe===
*{{Flagu|France}} – French President [[François Hollande]] sent a telegram to Macri and expressed "We will have the opportunity at that time to deepen our dialogue and our bilateral relationship that is one of the densest known to the Latin American continent". Hollande also confirmed a state visit to Argentina in February 2016.<ref>{{cite news|title=François Hollande felicitó a Macri por su triunfo y confirmó que vendrá al país en febrero|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1849879-hollande-felicito-a-macri-por-su-triunfo-y-confirmo-que-vendra-al-pais-en-febrero|access-date=29 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=29 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Germany}} – German Chancellor [[Angela Merkel]] also congratulated Macri and requested that he make a state visit. She added that the two countries have "always been deeply tied", particularly in the area of science which she deemed "one of the pillars" of the two countries' relations. Merkel also remarked that she would be "thankful" if the countries could strengthen cooperation "in all areas".<ref>{{cite news|title=Angela Merkel felicitó a Mauricio Macri y lo invitó a Alemania|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848404-angela-merkel-invito-a-mauricio-macri-a-alemania|access-date=24 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=24 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Italy}} – Italian Prime Minister [[Matteo Renzi]] called Macri on the night of his victory and stated that he will meet soon with the new president to "open a new page of collaboration between the two countries". He also highlighted the [[Italian Argentine|historical and cultural ties]] between the two countries, stating that "it is the country with the largest presence of Italian citizens in the world", numbering some 900,000. The Cambiemos victory also provoked much reaction in the domestic Italian press.<ref name="Piqué">{{cite news|last1=Piqué|first1=Elisabetta|title=Matteo Renzi llamó a Macri para felicitarlo|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848123-italia-mauricio-macri-ballottage-presidente|access-date=23 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Russia}} – In a telegram to Macri, Russian President [[Vladimir Putin]] expressed his hopes that the two countries will continue to increase the "bilateral cooperation within diverse areas and the coordination of efforts to resolve current occurrences within the international agenda", adding that "the fundamental intesests of the people of Russia and Argentina contribute to guarantee the stability and security of Latin America and the world", while reminding Macri that the countries had recently celebrated 130 years of diplomatic relations. Putin also made reference to the ongoing nuclear power and hydrocarbon extraction projects between the countries.<ref>{{cite news|title=Putin le mandó un telegrama a Macri para saludarlo por el triunfo|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848144-putin-macri-presidente-ballottage|access-date=23 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Spain}} – Spanish Prime Minister [[Mariano Rajoy]], who has a close relationship with Macri, congratulated him and invited him to carry out a state visit "as soon as possible", stating that he is confident that the new government will "lead this new stage with success" while offering "the necessary support to consolidate the historical ties of friendship, fraternity and cooperation". The relationship between Spain and Argentina had become increasingly tense under the presidency of Cristina Kirchner, particularly after the [[Renationalization of YPF]] in 2012.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Rodríguez Yebra|first1=Martín|title=Mariano Rajoy felicitó a Mauricio Macri y lo invitó a España|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848091-mariano-rajoy-felicito-a-mauricio-macri-y-lo-invito-a-espana|access-date=23 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015|ref=Yebra}}</ref>
*{{Flagu|United Kingdom}} – UK Prime Minister [[David Cameron]] called Macri to congratulate him and offered his support for his presidency. A [[Downing Street]] spokesperson stated that "both leaders expect to meet in the near future", emphasising trade relations and investments, while also prioritising the establishment of a free trade agreement between [[MERCOSUR]] and the [[European Union]] "as soon as possible".<ref>{{cite news|title=Cameron llamó a Macri para felicitarlo y ofrecerle apoyo|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1849119-cameron-llamo-a-macri-para-felicitarlo-y-ofrecerle-apoyo|access-date=26 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=26 November 2015}}</ref>

===Financial sector===
The [[MERVAL]] index climbed 28% to record highs in the four weeks leading up to the run-off, largely attributed to a potential Cambiemos victory, though this dropped 3% on the day following the election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Buenos Aires shares cut early gains|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/203601/argentine-assets-jump-one-day-after-runoff|access-date=24 November 2015|work=Buenos Aires Herald|date=23 November 2015}}</ref> [[JPMorgan Chase|JPMorgan]] lowered Argentina's risk index by 16% on the day following the election, to levels not seen since 2011, while Argentine bonds increased across the board.<ref>{{cite news|title=Tras la victoria de Macri, el riesgo país cae a niveles del 2011|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1848126-tras-la-victoria-de-macri-el-riesgo-pais-cae-a-niveles-del-2011|access-date=24 November 2015|work=La Nacion|date=23 November 2015}}</ref> Similarly, [[Moody's Investors Service|Moody's]] raised the country's outlook from "stable" to "positive" following the election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Argentina outlook lifted to positive at Moody's|url=http://www.ft.com/fastft/430571/moodys-raised-outlook-on-argentina|access-date=24 November 2015|work=Financial Times|date=24 November 2015}}</ref>

